"Lesson 3 ended with a promise: logic gates, which only answer yes or no, can add. That sounds odd. AND, OR, and NOT know nothing about numbers. But Lesson 1 showed that a number is only a row of switches, and adding is a job you can do one column at a time. If gates can handle one column, a row of them can handle the whole sum.": "5d2e438b2f1a",
"Lesson 3 ended with a promise: logic gates, which only answer yes or no, can add. That sounds odd. AND, OR, and NOT know nothing about numbers. But Lesson 1 showed that a number is only a row of switches, and adding is a job you can do one column at a time. If gates can handle one column, a row of them can handle the whole sum. Start with the smallest problem: add two bits. There are only four cases. 0 + 0 is 0, 0 + 1 is 1, 1 + 0 is 1, and 1 + 1 is two, which in binary is written 10: write 0 and carry 1. Now look at what you write down in each case: 0, 1, 1, 0. That is the table for XOR. Look at the carry: 0, 0, 0, 1. That is the table for AND. So one XOR gate and one AND gate, side by side, add two bits. This small circuit is called a half adder. A half adder is enough for the column on the far right. Every other column has a third number to add: the carry coming in from its right. A circuit that adds three bits is called a full adder. It can be built from two half adders and an OR gate. Its hardest case is 1 + 1 + 1, which is three, written 11 in binary: write 1 and carry 1. Now give every column its own full adder, and connect each carry out to the carry in of the column on its left. That is a complete adding machine. Try 0111 + 0001 in the model. The right column makes a carry, the next column passes it on, and so does the next, until it lands in the fourth column: 1000. This is the same falling-domino carry you saw in Lesson 1. Because the carry ripples along, engineers call this a ripple-carry adder. People built adding machines long before electronics. About 1642, the French mathematician Blaise Pascal designed one that added by turning wheels, to help his father, a tax collector. Nearly three hundred years later, George Stibitz of Bell Laboratories took two telephone relays, which are switches worked by electricity, some flashlight bulbs, and strips cut from a tobacco can, and made a circuit that added two binary digits. He built it at home on his kitchen table, so it became known as the Model K. The adder inside a processor today works on many more bits at once than our four: 16, 32, or 64, depending on the computer. Its designers also use clever circuits that work out the carries ahead of time, so the answer does not have to wait for a long ripple. The idea has not changed. This is also the end of Unit 1. You started with one switch, on or off. A row of switches became a number. Codes turned numbers into words, pictures, and sound. Gates answered questions, and now gates add. Everything else a computer does is built from these few ideas.": "d6366a3b20db",
"Now give every column its own full adder, and connect each carry out to the carry in of the column on its left. That is a complete adding machine. Try 0111 + 0001 in the model. The right column makes a carry, the next column passes it on, and so does the next, until it lands in the fourth column: 1000. This is the same falling-domino carry you saw in Lesson 1. Because the carry ripples along, engineers call this a ripple-carry adder.": "f652a0b110ad",
